      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Verified every field against the 12/6/24 text of 247 CMR 9.00 and 2.00, Board Policy 2020-05, 21 CFR parts 7 and 203, FDA's recall page and 21 U.S.C. 331, 351, 352 and 353. The 9.01(7) mandatory and discretionary limbs, the quarantine-and-dispose sentence, 9.01(5) and 9.01(6), the 2.00 dispensing definition, the 9.13(1)-(3) container, label, policy and lot-confirmation conditions, the 9.19(5) stock label, 9.19(12), the 9.16(1) two-year records, and 9.22(1)-(5) with its one-year records are all stated accurately and with the right actor and modality. The Policy 2020-05 ranges (36-46°F / 2-8°C; -13 to 14°F / -25 to -10°C), twice-daily review, closed-day mechanism and six-step excursion plan (including notifying the patient and the patient's provider) are accurate and presented as Board guidance. The section flags that the policy's non-auto-defrost allowance conflicts with 9.22(2)'s 'unless otherwise approved by the Board' and gives a safe course. Part 7 is presented as guidance, with 7.49(d) worded as 'should'. Classes, depth, market withdrawal and stock recovery are correct, and no patient-level duty is invented. The 331(c), (k) and (t) prohibited acts, the 351 and 352 grounds, the 353(b)(2) label elements, the surviving 352 paragraphs (including repealed (k) and (l)) and the 353(c)-(d)/part 203 sample channel (retail pharmacy excluded from health care entity) are accurate. The section makes no unsupported claim that expired drugs are adulterated or misbranded. All eight practice questions are answerable from the section and not contradicted by it. One Low wording imprecision in common_traps[11] (9.01(12) cited without its recipient qualifier) does not mislead. Publishable as it stands."
